Cloud Computing allows businesses to consume computing resources (servers, storage, networks) over the internet on demand instead 
of running applications on a single local computer or physical server in a single country. This makes applications highly 
accessible, reliable, cost-effective, and ready to grow without requiring expensive up-front hardware investments.

Evolution of cloud computing :- 
1960s: Physical Servers---> 1999: Virtualization (alerady noted in previous topics) --> 2006: Automation & Mgmt --> 2012+ - Present: Modern Cloud

types of cloud computing :- 
  1. Public Cloud: Shared infrastructure owned and managed by third-party vendors (AWS, Azure, Google Cloud, Alibaba, Oracle) accessed over the
  public internet. Cost-effective and highly scalable.
  2. Private Cloud: Dedicated cloud infrastructure built exclusively for a single organization (e.g., banks, healthcare, government). Offers supreme control,
  security, and compliance.
  3. Hybrid Cloud: A combined environment of both public and private clouds that can work together, sharing data and applications as needed.

types of Cloud Service Models :-
    1. IaaS (Infrastructure as a Service) - Rent basic computing building blocks like servers, storage, and networking. You have full control over
       the Operating System (OS) and applications.
    2. PaaS (Platform as a Service) - Rents a pre-configured environment to build, test, and deploy applications. The vendor manages the
       underlying OS, networking, and servers so you can focus entirely on coding.
    3. SaaS (Software as a Service) - Access a complete, ready-to-use software application over the internet. No installation,
       maintenance, or infrastructure management is required.

  example of IaaS - Amazon EC2, Virtual Machines(VMs)
  example of PaaS - Heroku, Google App Engine
  example of SaaS - Gmail,Zoom, miocrosoft 365
